Well, Nethack is certainly not the best primer for someone who is 
learning to program. Without meaning to be rude to any of the DevTeam,
the source is a bit of a dog's breakfast. It has been hacked on by
very many people (including me) over very many years, in no principled 
fashion. It's pretty amazing that it works as well as it does.

>I am making the assumption that it is in C++, which is what I am
>currently learning.

It's in plain old C, actually. Every now and then someone suggests
that it should be rewritten in an OO language, but the task is far 
too huge for anyone to seriously contemplate.

>Does anyone know of a guide to working with this source code, some kind
>of tutorial?

There's Rob Ellwood's guide:

http://members.shaw.ca/rob.ellwood/HowToChangeNethack.html

and German Martin's guide:

http://cvs.sourceforge.net/cgi-bin/viewcvs.cgi/slashem/slashem/doc/sources.txt?rev=HEAD&content-type=text/vnd.viewcvs-markup

and Topi Linkala has annotated some of the source:

http://koti.welho.com/tlinkala/nethack/Annotated_nethack_code.html

>Does anyone already experienced with working on Nhack or any Rogue-like
>source have advice for someone just starting out?

Yes. My best advice is: start someplace else. But make sure you come
back once you know what you're doing, because the more patch-makers
there are, the merrier.

